      "content": "Wait, what does \"no on the federal reserve\" mean? I think you got mixed up there lol. \n\nBut anyway, you're saying the \"true cost\" is the real issue. So if the data shows that vouchers actually help some kids succeed, isn't it kind of unfair to keep blocking them just to protect a failing status quo? Like, if we know a kid can do better elsewhere, shouldn't we just prioritize their individual success over a broken system?",
